WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.314
A brushless drive usually splits its loop between firmware and a separate gate driver.

00:00:07.314 --> 00:00:09.470
SKU-1 puts both on one die.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.944
and keeps the loop in hardware.

00:00:12.994 --> 00:00:17.637
Commands arrive from the host, and the processor sets the loop up.

00:00:17.637 --> 00:00:23.899
But it never sits inside the loop, so firmware timing never decides when a gate switches.

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:34.080
The torque command turns into three phase drive in fixed hardware, so the loop's latency is known, with a target under 1 microsecond.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:43.335
Seven PWM channels switch three half bridges, on a rail anywhere from 5 to 120 volts.

00:00:43.335 --> 00:00:46.714
That voltage is why the die is BCD.

00:00:47.764 --> 00:00:54.896
Where the rotor is, and how much current flows in each phase, both return straight to the regulator.

00:00:54.896 --> 00:00:57.107
Firmware is never in that path.

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:02.494
On a thermal, overcurrent or overvoltage fault, the gates shut.

00:01:02.494 --> 00:01:06.694
And they stay shut, latched, until someone decides otherwise.

00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:09.415
SKU-1 is pre-silicon.

00:01:09.415 --> 00:01:11.403
Every figure is a target.

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:19.916
The converter's isolation from switching edges, and the loop deadline under bus load, must still be proven.

00:01:19.916 --> 00:01:22.779
Bring us your motor and your bridge.

00:01:22.779 --> 00:01:25.882
We will tell you what SKU-1 must prove.
